  "summary": "A painting technique using water-soluble pigments suspended in a water-based medium.",
  "description": "Aquarelle emphasizes transparency and luminosity through layers of thin washes, allowing the white paper to show through. It developed as a respected fine art medium in Europe during the Renaissance, moving beyond its initial use as a preparatory medium.",
  "culturalContext": "Aquarelle — European watercolor's high tradition — grew from topographical survey drawing (Cotman, Girtin) into romantic expressive wash: Turner's atmosphere experiments, Sargent's Venetian confidence, Zbukvic's modern plein-air grammar. Water's agency (blooms, backruns, granulation) makes the medium a collaboration with accident; the English watercolor school made it a national identity, and its white-paper luminosity remains the standard all \"light-filled\" media are measured against.",
